WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.550
For a long time, information on the Breaking
Bad movie was practically as elusive as Walt

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.700
and Jesse themselves.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.100
Now, we've gathered every last shard of information
we could get our hands on while we wait for

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.140
El Camino to finish cooking and finally crystallize
on our screens.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:19.270
After months spent referring to the Breaking
Bad film using the semi-secret codename "Greenbriar,"

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:23.280
Netflix finally revealed the movie's actual
name in August of 2019.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.930
The film will be called El Camino: A Breaking
Bad Movie, and will hit Netflix on October

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:28.980
11, 2019.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.460
Eventually, El Camino will also reportedly
air on AMC, Breaking Bad's home network, but

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.700
no date has been announced yet for the film's
cable debut.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.130
Until Netflix announced the October release
date for El Camino, very little was known

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:45.160
about the movie, although Bob Odenkirk implied
that filming had already been completed when

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.670
he talked to The Hollywood Reporter promoting
the upcoming fifth season of the Breaking

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.840
Bad spinoff Better Call Saul, on which Odenkirk
stars.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.400
When asked if the film was in fact happening,
Odenkirk replied,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.930
"I find it hard to believe you don't know
it was shot.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:57.930
They did it.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:58.930
You know what I mean?

00:00:58.930 --> 00:00:59.930
How is that a secret?

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:00.930
But it is.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.420
They've done an amazing job of keeping it
a secret."

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.610
If secrecy was the goal, Netflix and the Breaking
Bad team definitely succeeded.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:11.060
Mere days after Odenkirk gave his interview,
exciting fans with the possibility that filming

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.649
might already be completed, Netflix announced
a release date less than two months away.

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:19.429
After months of uncertainty about whether
a movie was even happening, the Breaking Bad

00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:22.680
film was practically on our doorstep, and
we hadn't even realized it was coming.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.880
Although Aaron Paul spent the better part
of a year playing his cards extremely close

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.430
to his chest, numerous sources speculated
early on that he'd be reprising his role as

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.080
Jesse in the eventual Breaking Bad film, and
that the film would revolve around him.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.180
Now those suspicions are confirmed.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.420
Breaking Bad saw Jesse evolve from a high
school dropout who taught Walt how to deal

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:45.380
drugs into the heart and soul of the show,
holding fast to the conscience and morality

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.760
that Walt slowly and methodically stripped
away.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.399
As Walt became more ruthless, Jesse seemed
to balance him by becoming increasingly more

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:52.940
sympathetic.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.520
The final season of Breaking Bad led to Jesse
getting kidnapped by a gang of neo-Nazis and

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.700
forced to cook crystal meth for them, until
Walt ultimately came to his rescue in the

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.240
finale.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.880
The last we saw of Jesse, he was driving away
from the neo-Nazis' meth lab in a stolen car,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.020
laughing in relief at the endless possibilities
granted to him by his long-awaited freedom.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.980
Despite being the star of the series, it's
unsurprising that Walter White may not play

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.460
a role in the movie, due to his death in the
series finale — he's hit by a stray bullet

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.170
when he initiates his plan to mow down the
neo-Nazis that have captured Jesse, and bleeds

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.080
out in a meth lab.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.760
Still, it's hard to imagine any version of
Breaking Bad without Cranston, who was the

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.720
indisputable star of the series and brought
one of the most memorable TV characters of

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.060
the 21st century to life in Walter White.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:39.340
"This is not meth."

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.880
While it appears as though El Camino will
take place after Walt's death, Cranston has

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.790
said that if asked to return, he would most
definitely do it.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.960
He told The Dan Patrick Show in November 2018:

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.600
"I don't know if there's an appearance — flashbacks,
flash forward, but I'm excited about it because

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.380
it's Breaking Bad and it was the greatest
professional period of my life and I can't

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.560
wait to see all those people again, even if
I just come by to visit."

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:11.100
It's hard to imagine how a flash forward could
allow Cranston to come back, but a flashback

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.000
— or even a dream or hallucination — could
be a reasonable way for Walt to make an appearance

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.840
in the film, even if he's not the star.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.580
Whatever the method, we hope El Camino finds
a way to work Walt in, even if it is just

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:22.100
for a single scene.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.160
Although Bob Odenkirk seems to be more in
the loop than most people regarding El Camino,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.280
he still speaks about the film as though it's
simply something he's heard about, and not

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.680
something he's worked on.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.140
Speaking to The Hollywood Reporter, Odenkirk
said:

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.930
"I've heard so many different things about
it, but I am excited about the Breaking Bad

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.480
movie.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.480
I can't wait to see it."

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.440
Of course, as any Breaking Bad fan could tell
you, no one is better at selling a lie than

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.960
Odenkirk, who plays the slippery lawyer Saul
Goodman — or Jimmy McGill, back before he

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.860
changed his name — on both the original
series and the spinoff, Better Call Saul.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:52.880
"Dude's like Houdini.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.300
Seriously, when the going gets tough, you
don't want a criminal lawyer.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.020
You want a criminal lawyer.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:01.640
Know what I'm saying"

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.400
If he was trying to throw us off the scent
and he actually is in El Camino, we'd probably

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.720
never even suspect the lie until his face
suddenly showed up on our TV screens.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.420
So while right now we're willing to take Odenkirk
at his word that he's merely eagerly awaiting

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.439
El Camino as a fan, just like the rest of
us, we also wouldn't be too surprised if he

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.440
has a supporting role after all.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.760
"Joke's on me, HA HA.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.460
Simple as that."

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.400
We last saw Jesse Pinkman driving off into
the night after Walt freed him from his abductors

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.180
at the end of "Felina."

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.080
Jesse never gave any indication about where
he might have been headed, but according to

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.560
the logline of El Camino, we're about to find
out.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.720
Netflix promises us:

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:40.199
"In the wake of his dramatic escape from captivity,
Jesse must come to terms with his past in

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:41.830
order to forge some kind of future."

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:46.180
This seems to indicate that El Camino — presumably
titled after the type of car Jesse drives

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.120
in this final scene — is about more than
just Jesse's physical journey away from his

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.580
kidnappers, but will address his emotional
one as well.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.789
The part about "[coming] to terms with his
past" almost definitely refers to the repercussions

00:04:56.789 --> 00:04:58.720
of Jesse's illegal actions with Walt.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:04.060
In the final season, Jesse agreed to cooperate
with DEA agent Hank to incriminate Walt, in

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.720
exchange for leniency for himself.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.610
However, Hank is murdered before their deal
can ever be finalized, making Jesse a fugitive

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.040
by the conclusion of the series.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.680
Jesse may also have to wrestle with some personal
demons, such as the deaths of his ex-girlfriends

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.449
Jane and Andrea, who both died because of
their involvement with Jesse.

00:05:20.449 --> 00:05:24.550
Perhaps one way Jesse will deal with the consequences
of his actions will be by going to check on

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.760
Andrea's orphaned son, Brock, and making sure
he is all right.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.669
Even before the logline of El Camino was revealed,
Breaking Bad fans already had reason to suspect

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:37.639
that any potential film would have to take
place after the finale of the series.

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:41.560
Not only were rumors already swirling around
the possibility of a Jesse-centric film, but

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.150
there's simply no other time in the Breaking
Bad narrative where a feature-length story

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:45.780
might fit.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.940
Walt and Jesse meet in the pilot of the series.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.419
Before that, Jesse is a small-time drug dealer
and Walt is a high school chemistry teacher

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.300
— hardly the stuff movies are made of.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.400
"It is fascinating, really,"

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.220
After that, the tale of Breaking Bad was so
tightly woven that it didn't seem possible

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:08.680
that there were another two minutes of relevant
story that we didn't see play out during the

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.090
course of the series, let alone two hours.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:14.560
There was even an entire episode dedicated
to getting a fly out of their lab.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:15.740
We saw everything.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.960
"Okay, on three.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:22.760
One…"

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:24.640
"Ah, f--!”

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:29.450
Cranston confirmed the post-finale time frame
in an interview with ET, where he said that

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:33.460
he's "still dead" in the film, and the official
logline also indicates that El Camino will

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.240
be picking up right where the finale left
off.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.120
However, if it's been a while since you've
watched Breaking Bad and you're worried you

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.499
won't remember enough to understand El Camino,
Aaron Paul has suggested you rewatch only

00:06:43.499 --> 00:06:47.779
one scene to prepare yourself for the film
— Jesse's heartbreaking monologue from season

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.320
three.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.580
"I have nothing.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:51.720
No one.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.640
Alright?

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.700
It's all gone.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:59.760
Get your tissues ready, because if that scene
is any indication of what El Camino will be

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:00.760
like… ouch.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:06.120
In the process of announcing the name and
premiere date of El Camino, Netflix also dropped

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.180
a teaser trailer.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.599
If it's been a while since you've watched
Breaking Bad, you may not recognize the character

00:07:10.599 --> 00:07:14.560
at the center of the teaser, but he's someone
who's been around since the very first season

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.200
of the show.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.259
In the short clip, which is set in a police
station, we hear a man telling a roomful of

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.980
officers that he doesn't have any idea where
"he" is or where "he" is headed.

00:07:23.289 --> 00:07:26.660
The camera then pivots around to reveal the
speaker: Skinny Pete.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:30.800
Skinny Pete was introduced in season one of
Breaking Bad as the one responsible for introducing

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.020
Jesse to drug kingpin Tuco Salamanca.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:38.220
Skinny Pete later deals crystal for Jesse,
sobers up, relapses, and ultimately helps

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:40.979
Walt seek revenge against his former business
partner.

00:07:40.979 --> 00:07:45.319
Now it seems as though the DEA may think that
Skinny Pete is the key to locating Jesse,

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:49.340
but despite their rocky relationship, Skinny
Pete shows no sign of turning on his friend.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:51.300
"I don't know what to tell you.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.360
But no way I'm helping you people put Jesse
Pinkman back inside a cage."

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:00.919
Breaking Bad would never have become the critical
and cultural smash it was without the vision

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:04.800
of Vince Gilligan, who not only created the
show, but was responsible for penning some

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.560
of its most memorable episodes, including
"Box Cutter," "Face Off," and the series finale,

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:10.080
"Felina."

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.880
Back when the news first broke that a feature-length
follow-up may be in the works, fans were relieved

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.660
to hear that it would, once again, have Gilligan
at the helm.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.029
While Gilligan did not write or direct every
episode of Breaking Bad, it looks as though

00:08:21.029 --> 00:08:23.150
he'll be doing both duties for El Camino.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:26.479
This won't be the first time Gilligan has
ventured back into the story of Jesse and

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.919
Walter since the show wrapped in 2013.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.500
He is also the creator of the ongoing spin-off
series Better Call Saul, which premiered two

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.560
years after the conclusion of Breaking Bad.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:39.100
After being immersed in this world for more
than a decade, there's no one better than

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.620
Gilligan to bring Jesse and the tattered remains
of Walt's once-great meth empire back onto

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:44.160
our screens.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:49.580
On the heels of the Breaking Bad finale, Gilligan
told GQ his theory about what happens to Jesse

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:51.170
after the events of "Felina."

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:51.700
He said:

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.500
"My personal feeling is that he got away.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.860
But the most likely thing, as negative as
this sounds, is that they're going to find

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:00.389
this kid's fingerprints all over this lab
and they're going to find him within a day

00:09:00.389 --> 00:09:01.320
or a week or a month.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.920
And he's still going to be on the hook for
the murder of two federal agents.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:08.960
But yeah, even though that's the most likely
outcome, the way I see it is that he got away

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.860
and got to Alaska, changed his name, and had
a new life.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.200
You want that for the kid.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:13.820
He deserves it."

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.840
The murders Gilligan is talking about are
of Walt's brother-in-law Hank Schrader and

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.490
his partner Steven Gomez, who were both killed
in the final few episodes of Breaking Bad.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.329
Of course, Jesse didn't commit either murder
— Hank and Gomie were killed in a shootout

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:30.540
with the neo-Nazis Walt hired to kill Jesse
when he thought Jesse was stealing the money

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.019
he'd buried in the desert — but the DEA
has no way of knowing that.

00:09:34.019 --> 00:09:37.860
Assuming Gilligan hasn't changed his mind
in the years since Breaking Bad ended, it

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.490
sounds like we can hopefully look forward
to a happy ending...hopefully.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:45.130
The only other options are that Jesse dies,
or he winds up in a drab prison cell being

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:49.230
watched by ranks of faceless guards for the
rest of his life, all for murders he didn't

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:49.740
commit.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.140
That sounds pretty, um, what's the word we're
looking for here?

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.440
"Sounds kind of Kafkaesque.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:01.620
Yeah, totally Kafkaesque."

